	5. Package Management:
		5.1 rpm:
			I. Low level package manager. i is for install, q is for query and e is for uninstall
			II. Does not resolve dependencies and only targets the spesified package
		5.2 yum
			I. High level package manager
			II Does resolve dependencies and installs them
		5.3 Local or remote repositories
			I. There exist multiple repositories which yum or rpm application goes out to fetch packages
			II. The addresses of said repositories are saved in the local system in the /etc/yum.repod.d/ directory.
			III. To be able to use other repos we just need to add their addresses to the configuration file.
		5.4 Upgrading repos
			I. Repositories may not keep up with some software update schedule and fall back.
			II. To resolve this we just need to use the official documentation of said software
		5.5 Lab:
			I. Using "rpm -q ansible" we can learn if rpm was used to download said package
			II. Using "sudo dnf install maven" to install maven package
			III. Use "rpm -e ansible" to remove a package
	6. Services:
		6.1 Services are processes which run in the background and are able to automatically start the affiliated binaries according to
		their configuration files.
			I.  Service files are located at /etc/systemd/system/ directory
		6.2 systemctl binary used to interact with the linux services also known as daemons. 
			I. Some commands are enable, disable, start, stop and status.
		6.3 There are 3 categories in a service file which are unit, service and install 		
			I. man systemd.service may be used for help
